HOUSE ROBBER III

(LeetCode 337)

🟢 Problem

You are given a binary tree.

Each node has money.

You cannot rob two directly-connected houses.

Return the maximum amount you can rob.

🔎 Example
        3
       / \
      2   3
       \    \
        3    1


Output:

7


Explanation:
Rob 3 (root) + 3 (left.right) + 1 (right.right) = 7

❌ Why Normal DFS Fails?

Because decision at one node depends on parent.

If you rob current node → you cannot rob children.

So we need to remember state.

🧠 Core Idea (State DP on Tree)

At each node, return two values:

int[2]

res[0] = max money if we DO NOT rob this node
res[1] = max money if we DO rob this node

🟢 Transition

Let:

left = dfs(node.left)
right = dfs(node.right)

🔹 Case 1: Rob current node

If we rob this node:

Cannot rob children.

rob = node.val + left[0] + right[0]

🔹 Case 2: Do NOT rob this node

If we don't rob:

We can choose best option from children

notRob = max(left[0], left[1]) +
         max(right[0], right[1])

Return:
return new int[]{notRob, rob}

🟢 Final Answer
int[] res = dfs(root)
return max(res[0], res[1])

🟢 Java Code
class Solution {

    public int rob(TreeNode root) {

        int[] result = dfs(root);

        return Math.max(result[0], result[1]);
    }

    private int[] dfs(TreeNode node) {

        if (node == null) return new int[]{0, 0};

        int[] left = dfs(node.left);
        int[] right = dfs(node.right);

        int rob = node.val + left[0] + right[0];

        int notRob = Math.max(left[0], left[1]) +
                     Math.max(right[0], right[1]);

        return new int[]{notRob, rob};
    }
}

🟢 Why This Works

We solve overlapping constraints using state.

Each node carries two states:

Robbed

Not robbed

Parent decides based on these states.

No global variable needed.

🟢 Time Complexity
O(n)


Each node visited once.

Space:

O(h)


Recursion stack height.
